# Security Review: `app.js`

## Executive Summary

The file is a minimal Express skeleton. In its current form it has **no business logic vulnerabilities** simply because it has almost no logic yet. However, it has **structural and foundational security gaps** that will become serious the moment the application grows. The findings below range from present-day issues to critical patterns that must be established now, before they become load-bearing.

---

## Finding 1 — No Security Headers (OWASP A05: Security Misconfiguration) 🔴 High

**Current code:**
```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
// No security headers configured at all
```

Express ships with no protective HTTP headers. Without them, browsers have no defense guidance and attackers can trivially exploit clickjacking, MIME-sniffing, and XSS reflection.

**Recommendation — add `helmet`:**
```js
const helmet = require('helmet');

app.use(helmet());                         // Sane defaults for all headers
app.use(helmet.hsts({ maxAge: 31536000 })); // Force HTTPS for 1 year
app.use(helmet.frameguard({ action: 'deny' })); // Block clickjacking
app.use(helmet.contentSecurityPolicy({
  directives: {
    defaultSrc: ["'self'"],
    scriptSrc:  ["'self'"],
    objectSrc:  ["'none'"],
    upgradeInsecureRequests: [],
  },
}));
```

---

## Finding 2 — No Rate Limiting (OWASP A07: Identification & Authentication Failures) 🔴 High

**Current code:**
```js
app.get('/users', (req, res) => { ... });
// Responds to unlimited requests with no throttle
```

Any endpoint without rate limiting is open to brute-force, credential-stuffing, scraping, and denial-of-service at zero cost to an attacker.

**Recommendation:**
```js
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const globalLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15-minute window
  max: 100,                  // Max 100 requests per window per IP
  standardHeaders: true,     // Return rate limit info in RateLimit-* headers
  legacyHeaders: false,
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later.' },
});

// Stricter limiter for any future auth endpoints
const authLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 10,
  message: { error: 'Too many login attempts.' },
});

app.use(globalLimiter);
// app.use('/auth', authLimiter);  // Apply when auth routes exist
```

---

## Finding 3 — No Authentication or Authorization (OWASP A01: Broken Access Control) 🔴 High

**Current code:**
```js
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    // ...
  ]);
});
```

`/users` returns PII (names and emails) to **any anonymous caller** with zero access control. There is no middleware, no token check, no role check.

**Recommendation — JWT middleware guard:**
```js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing or malformed token.' });
  }

  const token = authHeader.slice(7);
  try {
    req.user = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token.' });
  }
}

function requireRole(...roles) {
  return (req, res, next) => {
    if (!roles.includes(req.user?.role)) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Insufficient permissions.' });
    }
    next();
  };
}

// Apply to the route
app.get('/users', requireAuth, requireRole('admin'), (req, res) => { ... });
```

---

## Finding 4 — PII Exposed in Response (OWASP A02: Cryptographic Failures) 🟠 Medium

**Current code:**
```js
res.json([
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
]);
```

Email addresses are PII. The endpoint returns them to all callers, provides no field-level access control, and future developers may add fields (phone, address, password hash) to this object without realising the endpoint is already public. This also creates GDPR/CCPA exposure.

**Recommendation — explicit field allowlists:**
```js
function sanitizeUser(user) {
  // Only expose what this endpoint actually needs
  const { id, name } = user;
  return { id, name };
}

app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  const users = getUsersFromDb();           // Replace hardcoded data
  res.json(users.map(sanitizeUser));
});
```

